Coral reef {{18}}
An aerial view of the Weizhou Island, south China's Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region, March 20, 2026. Around Weizhou Island off the coast of south China's Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region, a 2,000-square-meter restoration demonstration zone built by a research team from Guangxi University's School of Marine Sciences saw live coral {{19}} rise from approximately 5% to 20% in just three years. "In 2023, with strong support from the Beihai municipal government and the Weizhou Island Management Committee, the team further deployed 1,520 artificial reefs and {{20}} more than 80,000 coral {{21}}, restoring 450 mu (about 30 hectares) of sea area," said Huang Wen, an associate professor at Guangxi University's School of Marine Sciences. Coral species increased from 46 to 62, and the area has become the only part of China's coastal waters where large whales are regularly sighted. Wildlife recovery
